{
	title: 'Shared green, red and yellow; one way to build themes',
	spec: 'src/common/colors.ts holds three shared values for any theme: colors.green [0.79, 0.145, 145.5] for success and addition, colors.red [0.73, 0.145, 26.5] for failure and removal, colors.yellow [0.86, 0.145, 95] for warnings. One chroma, so they read equally strong. The built-in look is grayscale except these, the blue [0.78, 0.145, 245] and orange [0.8, 0.145, 50] of a combined diff second column (hue 50 keeps orange apart from Hal amber), and Hal amber for the Hal home and percentages. Diff additions and removals, tab done, warning and error marks, and the context graph token kinds (statusCool, statusWarm, statusHot) use the shared values. Every theme is a Look of field overrides on the base, applied by themePlugin (src/client/theme-palette.ts); palette() is a shorthand that builds such a Look from seven colors and reads the shared values. A theme lists only fields that differ from the base: themes/hal.ts takes diff, exit, status token and tab status colors from it, so its diff and tab marks match (this replaces the separate stronger tab red of kx2, which out of sRGB looked like the diff red anyway). User approved in session 26-ess on 2026-10-28.',
